On Mon, 11 Dec 2006, Simon Richard Clarkstone wrote:
> Arthur J. O'Dwyer wrote:
>> http://www.contrib.andrew.cmu.edu/~ajo/free-software/nethack/wish343.diff
>> 
>> I just "finished" a patch to the wishing code that lets the player wish
>> for --- and get --- objects already existing in monsters' inventories,
>> as described back in 2003 here:
>> http://groups.google.com/groups?selm=Pine.LNX.4.55L-032.0308051326410.11206@unix50.andrew.cmu.edu 
>>   That is, if a dwarf is whaling on you with a pickaxe and you /really/
>> have no better way out, just #rub that lamp and wish for "the dwarf's
>> pickaxe". "e - a pickaxe. The dwarf looks startled!"
>
> How does it interact with not-normally-wishable-for items?  Does the 
> "already exists" or the "no-wishing" check get made first?  Be careful not 
> to unbalance the quest, though it would be nice to be able to steal stuff 
> back from covetous monsters.

   I believe it lets you wish for non-wishable items iff they are in
the possession of a monster on the current level, and you know the name
of that monster. I wasn't thinking about the Quest when I wrote the
code, so it's kind of serendipitous that you can't wish your Quest
object off the ground at the moment; thanks for alerting me to that
pitfall.
   Do you think it would really unbalance the game to let the player
recover the Quest item by using up one wish? I think of wishes as
insanely valuable, but then I have next to no experience with the late
game.


>>   * Wishing for things on the ground; but how do you specify them?
>> "That cockatrice corpse"?
>
> This brings to mind a generalisation possibility:
> "Wish for an item by typing the name? [ynq]"
> If you say yes, then you get the normal wishing prompt, if quit, then you 
> don't wish for anything, and if no, then you go to the second prompt:
> "Wish for an item by locating it? [ynq]"
> If you say no, then you go back to the first prompt, if quit, then you 
> don't wish for anything, but if you say yes:
> "Where is the item?"
> You use hklyubn<> to navigate and . to select.
> Then:
> "Which item?" + inventory list, or "Which item (type the name)?"

   You shouldn't be able to view monsters' inventories, or peek inside
closed containers, this way; I'd consider that a bug. That's why I
like the text-only interface.

> There would need to be a way to differentiate between a container and its 
> contents.  Also, pressing escape should mostly allow you to back out of 
> one question rather than cancelling the whole wishing process, in case you 
> forget which bag contains something.

   Yeah, that's the kind of abuse I mean. :)

> You know, this would work far better if there was a proper "ownership" 
> system.

   Yes. Then when a nymph steals your sword, you can wish for "my sword"
and get it back. (Actually, another improvement for my current patch is
to let "sword" match "long sword", and so on.)

-Arthur


